    "area": "Gold Butte National Monument, south of Mesquite",
    "signature": "The monument's main dirt artery.",
    "summary": "Gold Butte Road and the wash road beside it are the way into Gold Butte National Monument: sandstone, Joshua trees, and the Little Finland country south of Mesquite.",
    "why": "This is the Nevada monument most people miss on the way to Zion.",
    "highlights": [
      "Whitney Pockets area, if you detour on a designated road",
      "The road toward Little Finland",
      "Falling Man and the other rock art, from the road and marked trails only"
    ],
    "best": "October–April. Summer is brutal and the roads rut after rain.",
    "time": "44 miles · A full day from Las Vegas",
    "difficulty": "High clearance. Sandy washes. Not technical until you leave the main road, which you should not.",
    "elevation": "About 2,000–4,000 ft",
    "fee": "Gold Butte National Monument. Designated roads only. No dispersed camping in some zones. Pack out everything.",
    "permit": "A national monument, not an open riding area. Off-route driving is the fastest way to get the roads closed.",
    "access": "South from Riverside / Mesquite, or the scenic back way from the Lake Mead side. The last pavement ends and the monument begins.",
    "tips": [
      "Little Finland is fragile. Walk the last bit on the signed path.",
      "Carry water for a day even if you plan a shorter one."
    ],
