    "area": "East of Moab, under the La Sals",
    "signature": "A creek crossing road that earns the name.",
    "summary": "Onion Creek Road leaves the highway east of Moab and fords the creek again and again on the way toward the Fisher Towers and the La Sals. The water smells like sulfur. The canyon is narrow and red.",
    "why": "The best easy-to-moderate dirt drive in Moab, which means it is still a creek.",
    "highlights": [
      "The repeated crossings",
      "The narrows",
      "The road toward the towers"
    ],
    "best": "Spring through fall, and not during runoff or a storm. The crossings get deep.",
    "time": "9.3 miles · 2–4 hours",
    "difficulty": "High clearance. Four-wheel drive when the crossings are up. A low car becomes a boat.",
    "elevation": "About 4,000–5,000 ft",
    "fee": "BLM and county. No fee.",
    "access": "Off UT-128, the river road east of Moab.",
    "tips": [
      "If you cannot see the bottom of a crossing, you do not know the bottom.",
      "The road can close when the creek is high. Believe it."
    ],
    "watch": "Water depth and flash floods. The canyon collects weather from the mountains.",
    "status": "Do not drive it in a flood.",
