    "area": "Capitol Reef, north district",
    "signature": "Temples of stone at the end of a river ford.",
    "summary": "Cathedral Valley is the remote north end of Capitol Reef: the Temple of the Sun and the Temple of the Moon, reached by a high-clearance loop that fords the Fremont River. The ford is the gate. If you would not walk it, do not drive it.",
    "why": "The quietest famous landscape in southern Utah.",
    "highlights": [
      "The river ford",
      "Temple of the Sun and Temple of the Moon",
      "The Upper Cathedral Valley overlook spur"
    ],
    "best": "Spring and fall. The ford is often too deep in runoff. The clay is impassable when wet. The valley can be closed.",
    "time": "58 miles · A full day from Torrey. Longer if you respect the ford.",
    "difficulty": "High clearance. The ford and the wash roads are the difficulty. Not a rock-crawler trail.",
    "elevation": "About 5,000–7,000 ft",
    "fee": "Capitol Reef entrance. No extra permit for the drive. The campground is small and first-come.",
    "permit": "Check the river before you commit. The park posts the ford. A line on this map does not mean the crossing is open this afternoon.",
    "access": "From the Fremont River ford off UT-24 east of the visitor center, or from the Hartnet side off the Cathedral Valley road near the west. Ask a ranger which way the ford is running.",
    "tips": [
      "Walk the ford. Measure it. Turn around.",
      "The temples are the destination. You do not need to drive every spur."
    ],
    "watch": "The river, then the clay. People get stuck in that order.",
    "status": "Check the ford with the park. Do not cross blind.",
