    "id": "hole-in-the-rock",
    "name": "Hole-in-the-Rock Road",
    "region": "utah",
    "categories": [
      "trail"
    ],
    "lat": 37.3388,
    "lng": -111.08316,
    "area": "Grand Staircase-Escalante, south of Escalante",
    "signature": "Fifty-plus miles of washboard to a history you finish on foot.",
    "summary": "Hole-in-the-Rock Road runs south from Escalante toward the Colorado River, the route of the 1879 expedition. The road is long, corrugated, and usually not technical. The actual hole, the slot down to the river, is a hike. You do not drive the historic descent.",
    "why": "The spine of the Escalante backcountry. Every trailhead you wanted is a spur off it.",
    "highlights": [
      "Dance Hall Rock",
      "Devil's Garden",
      "The end of the road at the hole, and the walk down"
    ],
    "best": "March–November. After rain the spurs, not always the main road, become the problem. Winter can be fine and cold.",
    "time": "55 miles · A full day to the end and back. More if you walk.",
    "difficulty": "High clearance recommended. The main road is often a careful SUV. It is washboard the whole way. Spurs to trailheads can be worse.",
    "elevation": "From Escalante at about 5,800 ft down toward the river",
    "fee": "Grand Staircase-Escalante. No fee on the road. Trailhead toilets are primitive. Carry water.",
    "permit": "The historic wagon route down the hole is not a vehicle route. Stop at the end of the road.",
    "access": "South from UT-12 in Escalante. The road starts in town. You will think it is the wrong road. It is not.",
    "tips": [
      "Fuel and water in Escalante. There is nothing down the road but other people who also brought enough.",
      "The last ten miles feel longer than the first forty."
    ],
    "watch": "Washboard, soft shoulders, and side roads that require more vehicle than the main road. Do not drive down the hole.",
    "status": "The hole itself is a walk, not a continuation of the drive.",
    "pairWith": [
      "capitol-reef",
      "bryce"
    ],
