    "lng": -111.8769,
    "area": "Between the Verde Valley and I-17",
    "signature": "A desert basin road with a worse name than the drive.",
    "summary": "Bloody Basin Road crosses the country east of the Bradshaws, a long dirt road between the desert and the Verde Valley. It is a high-clearance scenic route, historic and empty, not a rock trail.",
    "why": "The Arizona drive people do when they are tired of red rocks and not yet ready for the Rim.",
    "highlights": [
      "The basin",
      "The remote middle, which is most of it"
    ],
    "best": "October–May. Summer is hot and the monsoon ruts it.",
    "time": "14 miles · Half a day to a full day",
    "difficulty": "High clearance. Rough dirt. Usually not technical.",
    "elevation": "About 3,000–5,000 ft",
    "fee": "Tonto and Prescott forest country. Stay on the road.",
    "access": "From I-17 south of Cordes Junction, or from the Cave Creek side. It is a through road if it is open.",
    "tips": [
      "Check both ends. A washout in the middle is a long U-turn.",
      "No services. The name is the only drama if you stay on the road."
    ],
